  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nA strong anticyclone is expected to persist near the SoCal coast \nthrough at least Thursday, which will keep temperatures well above \nnormal and near daily records across SoCal. Temperatures are also \nexpected to warm another 1-2 degrees across south-central AZ on \nThursday, following the passage of a weak shortwave trough. Even \nsouth-central AZ will see high temperatures up to 10 degrees above \nnormal (99-102 degrees) on Thursday. An Extreme Heat Watch remains \nin effect through Thursday for Imperial Valley, CA and Yuma, AZ, \nwhere probabilities of Major HeatRisk are highest (50-70%). This \nwatch is likely to be upgraded to a warning tonight or tomorrow \nmorning and Friday is being considered for the heat alert as well. \nLatest NBM forecast shows 30-50% Major HeatRisk probs on Friday for \nthe same areas under the current watch. \n\nThe coastal anticyclone is expected to breakdown heading into Friday \nas the synoptic pattern shifts, with a Pacific low moving toward the \nCA coast, but low level temperatures are expected to remain very \nwarm. Latest global ensembles show 850mb temps cooling by only a \ndegree or two centigrade into Friday, which should only result in 1-\n4 degrees of surface cooling from Thursday. Latest high temperature \nforecast for Friday has 101-106 degrees across southeast CA and \nsouthwest AZ to 97-101 degrees across south-central AZ.\n\nAs alluded to, the pattern will shift heading into the end of this \nweek and this weekend with a trend toward troffing and negative \nheight anomalies across the Western CONUS. While there is good \nconfidence in the trough pattern developing, there is still a lot of \nmodel uncertainty/spread regarding how the trough pattern will \nevolve heading into the end of the weekend and start of next week.\nIt does look like the Pacific low that will near the CA coast on \nFriday will dip south this weekend and interact or faze with TC \nRachel while at the same time a trough will move out of the Gulf of \nAlaska and strengthen as it moves south down the northern Pacific \ncoast. There are certainly a lot of eyes on what will happen with TC \nRachel and whether or not it will help bring rain to the Desert \nSouthwest, just like with TCs Odalys and Polo early last week. \n\nLatest guidance has trended toward an eventual turn to the northeast \nby Rachel by the end of the weekend. While this trend does lend \nitself in favor of a push of moisture toward the Southwest CONUS, \nthere is still a lot of uncertainty in the forecast this far out, \nespecially with timing and evolution of the trough moving down the \nnorthern Pacific coast and how that may affect the remnants of \nRachel. Current thinking is at least mid and high level moisture \nwill eventually shear off or Rachel up toward the Southwest, which \nmay bring showery activity as early as Sunday and then the more \npotent coastal trough/low will help drive additional shower and \nstorm chances across AZ, while also sending a cold front and cooler \ntemperatures through the region early next week. Again uncertainty \nis still high and it is ill-advised to buy into single deterministic \nlong-range model runs that may show a strong tropical circulation \npushing into the U.S.",
